I've written the assessment to session_plan/assessment.md and it's on disk for the planner. The commit step did nothing because session_plan/ is gitignored, which the || true in the command allows for.

One new bug, confirmed on the built binary, filed as #1216. If .yoyo/memory.json has a single JSON typo (I used a trailing comma), yoyo reads it as an empty store. The next /remember then overwrites the file, so every saved memory is erased and nothing warns the user. With the same file minus the comma, both entries were kept. The cause is src/memory.rs:77, which turns a parse error into an empty store. Claude Code fixed the same kind of bug today: in its 2.1.328 release, a file it couldn't read got erased the next time it was saved. The issue asks for a check of every yoyo store that is loaded, changed and saved, not just memory.json.

#1214 is confirmed on the binary. yoyo -p ... -o keep.txt and --save-session s.json both silently replaced files that already existed. One design point is in the assessment for the planner: --save-session is often used with --load on the same file, so replacing the file this run loaded has to stay quiet, the way /save works now.

State of things:
• The build was verified green at session start, the last 4 evolve runs succeeded, and there were no reverts in the window.
• Two files are close to the line-count gate, where going over fails cargo test and reverts the whole task. src/context.rs has 15 lines of headroom and is not on the size-exemption list. src/format/mod.rs has 14 lines before its recorded size goes FATAL.
• #1215 is still waiting on @yuanhao. Until he answers, the harness copies a plan's "Fixes #N" title onto the leftover commit, so the planner should only title a task "Fixes" if it will close the whole ticket.

Research: I applied my changelog-transfer method from yopedia to the latest Claude Code release and Codex 0.169/0.170, and saved the findings to yopedia. Four other transferable ideas are noted but not yet checked in yoyo:
• whether auto-compaction rebuilds the system prompt the way /compact does
• leaving out an MCP tool whose input schema is invalid, and telling the model why
• whether a hook that fails blocks the action or lets it through
• trust that doesn't widen when the sandbox does (Codex 0.169), which is related to #1188

My suggestion to the planner is #1216 and #1214 as the two tasks: both are small, product-facing, and confirmed on the binary.
